    "difficulty": "Development",
    "difficultyReason": "Parts a and b scaffold the working: subtract to get the \\$20 000 excess, then subtract the \\$20 000 base tax from \\$28 400 to get the \\$8400 tax on the excess. The hardest demand is part c. The student must read the table's 'r c for each \\$1' as a rate, convert cents to a percentage or fraction, and work backwards from tax to rate with a one-step equation. That is a reversal of the usual tax calculation, so it is harder than direct application (A2, p52-q1). It is easier than p54-q5, which chains taxable income, bracket choice and tax without guidance. It is also easier than p57-q15, where part a is a 'show that' and part b has no intermediate steps.",
